esync: a wait on the thread's own process or thread waits out its timeout

WoW waits on GetCurrentProcess() with a timeout to sleep. With esync, ntdll
passes pseudo-handles to the server, and the server can't wait on a process
or thread there: their sync object is an in-process one, whose add_queue is
no_add_queue. So the wait failed at once with STATUS_OBJECT_TYPE_MISMATCH and
WoW called it again, about 35,000 times a second from one thread. That thread
used about 45% of a core and Wine's server another 45%.

Such a wait can never be satisfied while the thread is waiting, so wait for
the timeout alone (or an APC when alertable), like Windows does.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/esync.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/esync.c
index efcab06..0d67dd1 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/esync.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/esync.c
@@ -944,6 +944,14 @@ static NTSTATUS __esync_wait_objects( DWORD count, const HANDLE *handles, BOOLEA
         ntdll_get_thread_data()->alert_fd = fd;
     }
 
+    /* A thread can never see its own process or thread signaled while it waits on it, and
+     * WoW waits on GetCurrentProcess() with a timeout as a way to sleep. The server can't
+     * wait on these here (their sync object lives in the client), so it failed the wait at
+     * once and the caller looped, about 35,000 times a second. Just wait out the timeout
+     * (or an APC, if alertable), which is what Windows does. */
+    if (count == 1 && (handles[0] == NtCurrentProcess() || handles[0] == NtCurrentThread()))
+        count = 0;
+
     NtQuerySystemTime( &now );
     if (timeout)
     {
